── 3. THE ON-CHAIN CALL, AND WHAT HAPPENS WHEN IT FAILS ─────────────────────

   SOURCE (node_modules/@zerodev/sdk/accounts/utils/toKernelPluginManager.ts:390-393):
     const validatorMode = !regular || (await isPluginEnabled(...)) ? DEFAULT : ENABLE
   :175-180  isEnabled = (await regular_.isEnabled(...)) || (await isPluginInitialized(...))
   node_modules/@zerodev/permissions/toPermissionValidator.ts:137-156
     isEnabled: try { readContract permissionConfig(pId) … } catch (error) { return false }
   toPermissionValidator.ts:86  address: zeroAddress   -> the isPluginInitialized
     disjunct calls a codeless address, throws, and is caught to false as well
     (isPluginInitialized.ts:11-23).
   => BOTH disjuncts collapse to FALSE on any error, so a FAILED READ YIELDS ENABLE.

   Subject: 0xa48ce91e2f3237e69660c1543042c007b8d33e75 (installed pId 0x3ca1cec8, deployed: true).
   This account's steady-state nonce is mode 0x00 (section 2). Now break ONLY
   its permissionConfig read — locally, inside our own transport, before the
   request leaves the process — and read the nonce again.

  3a healthy RPC                                 pId 0x3ca1cec8  perms 18  mode 0x00 vType 0x02  DEFAULT  permissionConfig calls 1  isFirstEnable false  PR#56 firstEnable false  ceiling 3,000,000
      ^ permissionConfig calls = 1 -> isPluginEnabled DOES hit the chain (yes, a real eth_call)
  3b permissionConfig read FAILS                 pId 0x3ca1cec8  perms 18  mode 0x01 vType 0x02  ENABLE   permissionConfig calls 1  isFirstEnable true   PR#56 firstEnable false  ceiling 3,000,000
      ^ VERDICT: a failed read makes the SDK claim ENABLE (0x01). FAIL-TOWARD-ENABLE.
  3b' RENEWAL + permissionConfig read FAILS      pId 0x4a0050b2  perms 18  mode 0x01 vType 0x02  ENABLE   permissionConfig calls 1  isFirstEnable true   PR#56 firstEnable false  ceiling 3,000,000
      ^ a renewal is ENABLE either way, so an RPC blip can never REFUSE one on this path.
        The blip only ever moves a STEADY-STATE op from DEFAULT to ENABLE (3b).
   3c WHOLE RPC down: account.getNonce() THREW — An unknown RPC error occurred.
      ^ so a total outage is loud (the executor's estimate would fail anyway).
        The dangerous case is 3b: the EntryPoint getNonce read succeeds while the
        permissionConfig read fails, which is one flaky call, not an outage.

── 3d. DOES MERRYMEN HAVE A DEPLOYED ACCOUNT WITH A LIVE WALL? ──────────────

   NO — and the section-2 subjects are therefore other people's accounts, not ours.
   merrymen's own account 0x032da6a0…9036 has executed exactly ONE UserOperation in
   its life: mode 0x00 / vType 0x00 (SUDO, DEFAULT), seq 0, block 51207025, success.
   Source: per-sender UserOperationEvent scan over all 2,208 ZeroDev-factory accounts
   on 4663 (spikes/first-op-gas/perm-ops.json, 0 accounts unreadable) — that account
   appears exactly once and never with vType 0x02.
   So no merrymen wall has ever been installed on chain, and the steady-state DEFAULT
   branch is demonstrated on the closest available subjects: real Kernel v3.3 accounts
   on the same chain whose permission validators DID land. That is a property of the
   Kernel/SDK mechanism, not of who owns the account.

── 4. WHAT worker/src/executor.ts ON fix/first-enable-gas COMPUTES ──────────
   const accountLive = await isDeployed();            // executor.ts:389
   const nonce       = await account.getNonce();      // executor.ts:390
   const firstEnable = !accountLive && isFirstEnable(nonce);  // :391
   const bounds      = firstEnable ? FIRST_ENABLE_GAS_BOUNDS : GAS_BOUNDS;  // :392
   (the tables above already print every one of those four values per case)

   ALSO NOTE executor.ts ~:325 — the estimate's stateOverride balance is
   `bounds.absoluteMax * feeCeiling * 2n`, i.e. sized FROM the chosen ceiling.
   So the 3,000,000 branch also hands the bundler too little imaginary ETH,
   and the refusal arrives as `gas-unreadable` (AA21) rather than `gas-absurd`.

── 5. boundGas()'s VERDICT — the repo's own function, run here ──────────────

   Gas inputs are the MEASURED walled first op on 4663, Pimlico, 2026-09-03
   (spikes/first-op-gas/renewal-override.ts): verif 7,418,031 · preVerif 243,380 · call 50,180.
   A deployed re-enable is the same op minus a ~360k deploy, so it is if anything
   SMALLER — the verdict below does not depend on that difference.

  undeployed first op -> FIRST_ENABLE_GAS_BOUNDS       ACCEPTED  signed total 9,677,123
  renewal, estimate SUCCEEDS -> GAS_BOUNDS             REFUSED   rule "gas-absurd"
      this operation wants 9677123 gas, past the 3000000 ceiling. Refused before signing — nothing was spent. A ceiling is crossed either because the operation is not the one it is meant to be, or because it genuinely needs more than we are willing to sign for; both are reasons to stop rather than to sign
  renewal, override too small -> estimate null         REFUSED   rule "gas-unreadable"
      the bundler would not estimate gas for this operation. That is a refusal to quote, not a quote of zero — submitting with limits we invented would risk paying for an operation that runs out of gas inside the EntryPoint.
  steady-state op (mode 0x00) -> GAS_BOUNDS            ACCEPTED  signed total 1,016,385

   (the steady-state row uses verificationGasLimit 657,108 — the SIGNED value on
    0xa48ce91e…'s post-enable ops, tx 0x66e4f2d6…, decoded from handleOps calldata.
    It is comfortably inside 3,000,000, which is what that ceiling is sized for.)

